1. vulkan-tutorial.com
Best starting point, hands down.

Walks you through everything from instance creation to a spinning textured cube
Written in C++ but the concepts translate directly to C
Very thorough explanations of why not just how
Free, online: vulkan-tutorial.com


2. The Khronos Vulkan Spec
The ground truth — if you need to know exactly what something does, this is it.

Every function, struct, enum documented precisely
Searchable and well organized
registry.khronos.org/vulkan/specs/latest/html/vkspec.html


3. Vulkan Guide (vkguide.dev)
Best second resource after the tutorial.

More practical and opinionated than vulkan-tutorial.com
Covers real engine patterns, not just hello triangle
Covers modern Vulkan (dynamic rendering, etc.)
vkguide.dev


4. Khronos Vulkan Samples
Official code samples from the Vulkan developers themselves.

Real working code for specific features
Good for looking up how a specific thing is done
github.com/KhronosGroup/Vulkan-Samples


5. GPU Info / gpuinfo.org
Not documentation but invaluable.

Shows you what extensions and features are actually supported across real hardware
Essential before relying on any extension
gpuinfo.org


Recommended Order

Start at vulkan-tutorial.com and build the triangle
Keep the Khronos spec open as a reference while you code
Move to vkguide.dev when you want to go beyond basics
Check gpuinfo.org whenever you want to use an extension
vulkan-tutorial use oop?11:52 AMYes, the tutorial uses C++ with some OOP — it wraps everything in a class:
cppclass HelloTriangleApplication {
public:
    void run() {
        initWindow();
        initVulkan();
        mainLoop();
        cleanup();
    }
private:
    VkInstance instance;
    VkDevice device;
    // ...
};
But it's pretty minimal OOP — it's really just using a class as a namespace to avoid globals. The actual Vulkan calls inside are all straight C-style function calls with structs, so the concepts translate directly to C.

If You Want Pure C Examples
vkguide.dev is more C-friendly in style, and for pure C there are some good GitHub resources:

github.com/nicebyte/nicegfx — C-style Vulkan
github.com/SaschaWillems/Vulkan — C++ but very straightforward, good for reference
You can also just follow vulkan-tutorial and mentally flatten the class into plain functions/globals — it's a 1:1 translation


The Translation is Simple
cpp// Tutorial C++
class App {
    VkInstance instance;
    void createInstance() { ... }
};
c// Your C equivalent
VkInstance instance;
void create_instance(void) { ... }
The OOP in the tutorial is shallow enough that it shouldn't get in your way at all.
